#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:2
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:4
msgid ""
"This page includes a number of terms that we use in our documentation, so"
" that you have a reference for how we're using them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:8
msgid "CI/CD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:10
msgid ""
"CI/CD is a common way to write *Continuous Integration and Continuous "
"Deployment*. In some scenarios, they exist as two separate platforms. "
"Read the Docs is a combined CI/CD platform made for documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:13
msgid "configuration file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:15
msgid ""
"YAML configuration file (e.g. ``.readthedocs.yaml``) that configures the "
"build process of your project on Read the Docs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:19
msgid ":doc:`/config-file/index`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:20
msgid "Practical steps to add a configuration file to your documentation project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:22
msgid "dashboard"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:24
msgid ""
"The \"admin\" site where Read the Docs projects are managed and "
"configured. This varies for our two properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:27
msgid "|com_brand|: https://app.readthedocs.com/dashboard/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:28
msgid "|org_brand|: https://app.readthedocs.org/dashboard/"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:29
msgid "default version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:31
msgid ""
"Projects have a *default version*, usually the latest stable version of a"
" project. The *default version* is the URL that is redirected to when a "
"users loads the `/` URL for your project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:33
msgid "diff"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:35
msgid ""
"A way to see the changes between two pieces of text, which shows the "
"added and removed content with a green and red color respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:37
msgid "discoverability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:39
msgid ""
"A documentation page is said to be *discoverable* when a user that needs "
"it can find it through various methods: Navigation, search, and links "
"from other pages are the most typical ways of making content "
"*discoverable*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:41
msgid "Docs as Code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:43
msgid ""
"A term used to describe the workflow of keeping documentation in a Git "
"repository, along with source code. Popular in the open source software "
"movement, and used by many technology companies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:47
msgid "flyout menu"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:49
msgid ""
"Menu displayed on the documentation, readily accessible for readers, "
"containing the list active versions, links to static downloads, and other"
" useful links. Read more in our :doc:`/flyout-menu` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:51
msgid "GitOps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:53
msgid ""
"Denotes the use of code maintained in Git to automate building, testing, "
"and deployment of infrastructure. In terms of documentation, GitOps is "
"applicable for Read the Docs, as the configuration for building "
"documentation is stored in ``.readthedocs.yaml``, and rules for "
"publication of documentation can be :doc:`automated </automation-rules>`."
" Similar to :term:`Docs as Code`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:59
msgid "maintainer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:61
msgid ""
"A *maintainer* is a special role that only exists on |org_brand|. The "
"creator of a project on |org_brand| can invite other collaborators as "
"*maintainers* with full ownership rights."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:64
msgid ""
"The *maintainer* role does not exist on |com_brand|, which instead "
"provides :doc:`/commercial/organizations`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:65
msgid "pinning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:67
msgid ""
"To *pin* a requirement means to explicitly specify which version should "
"be used. *Pinning* software requirements is the most important technique "
"to make a project :term:`reproducible`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:70
msgid ""
"When documentation builds, software dependencies are installed in their "
"latest versions permitted by the pinning specification. Since software "
"packages are frequently released, we are usually trying to avoid "
"incompatibilities in a new release from suddenly breaking a documentation"
" build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:75
msgid "Examples of Python dependencies::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:86
msgid "Read the Docs recommends using **exact pinning**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:88
#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:112
msgid "See: :doc:`/guides/reproducible-builds`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:89
msgid "pre-defined build jobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:91
msgid ""
"Commands executed by Read the Docs when performing the build process. "
"They cannot be overwritten by the user."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:93
msgid "project home"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:95
msgid ""
"Page where you can access all the features of Read the Docs, from having "
"an overview to browsing the latest builds or administering your project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:97
msgid "project page"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:99
msgid "Another name for :term:`project home`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:100
msgid "reproducible"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:102
msgid ""
"A documentation project is said to be *reproducible* when its sources "
"build correctly on Read the Docs over a period of many years. You can "
"also think of being *reproducible* as being *robust* or *resillient*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:105
msgid ""
"Being \"reproducible\" is an important positive quality goal of "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:107
msgid ""
"When builds are not reproducible and break due to external factors, they "
"need frequent troubleshooting and manual fixing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:110
msgid ""
"The most common external factor is that new versions of software "
"dependencies are released."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:113
msgid "root URL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:115
msgid ""
"Home URL of your documentation without the ``/<lang>`` and ``/<version>``"
" segments. For projects without custom domains, the one ending in "
"``.readthedocs.io/`` (for example, ``https://docs.readthedocs.io`` as "
"opposed to ``https://docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:118
msgid "slug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:120
msgid ""
"A unique identifier for a project or version. This value comes from the "
"project or version name, which is reduced to lowercase letters, numbers, "
"and hyphens. You can retrieve your project or version slugs from "
":doc:`our API <api/v3>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:124
msgid "static website"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:126
msgid ""
"A static site or static website is a collection of HTML files, images, "
"CSS and JavaScript that are served statically, as opposed to dynamic "
"websites that generate a unique response for each request, using "
"databases and user sessions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:129
msgid ""
"Static websites are highly portable, as they do not depend on the "
"webserver. They can also be viewed offline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:132
msgid "Documentation projects served on Read the Docs are *static websites*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:134
msgid ""
"Tools to manage and generate static websites are commonly known as "
"*static site generators* and there is a big overlap with documentation "
"tools. Some static site generators are also documentation tools, and some"
" documentation tools are also used to generate normal websites."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:138
msgid ""
"For instance, :doc:`Sphinx <sphinx:index>` is made for documentation but "
"also used for blogging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:139
msgid "subproject"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:141
msgid ""
"Project A can be configured such that when requesting a URL ``/projects"
"/<subproject-slug>``, the root of project B is returned. In this case, "
"*project B* is the subproject. Read more in :doc:`/subprojects`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:145
msgid "user-defined build jobs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:147
msgid ""
"Commands defined by the user that Read the Docs will execute when "
"performing the build process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:148
msgid "virtualenv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:150
msgid ""
"The default way for Python projects to create an isolated environment. "
"This ensures that a :doc:`reproducible set of dependencies </guides"
"/reproducible-builds>` are installed so that you project builds the same "
"way each time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:151
msgid "webhook"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:153
msgid ""
"A webhook is a special URL that can be called from another service, "
"usually with a secret token. It is commonly used to start a build or a "
"deployment or to send a status update."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:157
msgid "There are two important types of webhooks for Read the Docs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:159
msgid ""
"Git providers have webhooks which are special URLs that Read the Docs can"
" call in order to notify about documentation builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:160
msgid ""
"Read the Docs has a unique webhook for each project that the Git provider"
" calls when changes happen in Git."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:164
msgid ":doc:`/guides/setup/git-repo-manual`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:165
msgid "Manually configuration for Git repositories."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:167
msgid ":doc:`/build-notifications`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/glossary.rst:168
msgid "Receive notifications when your documentation builds fail."
msgstr ""

